@page "/download"
@using RavenNest.Blazor.Services.Models
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities
@using RavenNest.Blazor.Services
@inject NavigationManager NavigationManager
@inject AuthService AuthService

@*
    Every heading on this page used to sit outside the block it introduced. The panels centre
    themselves at a readable width and a bare h1 fills the section, so "System requirements" started
    about three hundred and fifty pixels to the left of the requirements. Each heading is inside its
    own panel now, which is how the dashboard has always done it, and the two cannot come apart.

    One section holding one column, rather than five sections. Five was what let site.css's
    `.front-page .section:nth-child(even) { background-color: black }` paint every other block, which
    only ever read as a stripe while sections spanned the whole window. And one column means the
    blocks are all the same width instead of each choosing its own and leaving the rest of the page
    empty next to it.

    The page is also ordered the way somebody actually does this: get the thing, set it up, then the
    details. Requirements were second, above the steps, which is a specification sheet standing
    between a person and the four things they have to do.
*@

<div class="top-section">
    <div class="hero-text">
        <h1>Run Ravenfall on your stream</h1>
        <p>Your viewers play from chat. There is nothing for them to install.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="white-section-break"></div>
<div class="page-content">
    <div class="section">
        <div class="rf-stack">

        <div class="rf-getstart">
            @*
                Windows is the supported build and Linux is a best effort, so they are not two equal
                cards. They were, which made an experimental build with no auto update look like an
                equally good choice.
            *@
            <div class="rf-getstart__main">
                <h2 class="rf-getstart__title">
                    <i class="fa-brands fa-windows" aria-hidden="true"></i> Download for Windows
                </h2>
                <p class="rf-getstart__body">
                    The supported build, 64 bit, with automatic updates. This is the one you want.
                </p>
                <a href="/download/latest" class="rf-btn rf-btn--primary rf-btn--tall">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-download" aria-hidden="true"></i> Download the client
                </a>
                <p class="rf-getstart__note">
                    Free, and it stays free. Setting it up takes about five minutes.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rf-getstart__alt">
                <h3 class="rf-getstart__subtitle">
                    <i class="fa-brands fa-linux" aria-hidden="true"></i> Linux x86/x64
                    <span class="rf-badge rf-badge--warn">Experimental</span>
                </h3>
                <p class="rf-getstart__body">
                    Not officially supported, and it has no working auto update. The link fails
                    outright if the current version had no successful Linux build.
                </p>
                <a href="/download/linux" class="rf-btn rf-btn--small">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-download" aria-hidden="true"></i> Download for Linux
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rf-prose">
            <h2 class="rf-panel__title">Setting it up</h2>
            <ol class="rf-steps">
                <li>
                    <b>Unzip the client.</b> Use <a href="https://www.7-zip.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7-Zip</a>
                    or similar and extract it into a folder of its own.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Set a password.</b> The client cannot sign in with Twitch, so it needs one of
                    its own: set it on the <a href="/password">password page</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Log in.</b> Open the client and sign in with your Twitch username and that
                    password. You should arrive in the village on Home island.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Join from chat.</b> Type <span class="rf-cmd">!join</span> in your own
                    channel. RavenfallOfficial replies and your character appears in the client.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p class="rf-meta">
                Alt and Enter leaves fullscreen if you would rather it ran in a window. Two factor
                authentication on your Twitch account is only needed if you intend to run your own
                bot rather than the official one, and
                <a href="https://help.twitch.tv/s/article/two-factor-authentication?language=en_US" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twitch explains that here</a>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rf-prose">
            <h2 class="rf-panel__title">What it needs to run</h2>
            @*
                Was a bullet list of three lines that each had to carry their own label. A facts
                list is the shape of a label and a value, and it puts the three where they can be
                compared against what you have.
            *@
            <dl class="rf-facts">
                <dt>Operating system</dt>
                <dd>64 bit Windows 10 or later. Linux is untested.</dd>

                <dt>Processor</dt>
                <dd>An i5-6700 or equivalent, with at least four cores.</dd>

                <dt>Graphics</dt>
                <dd>A GTX 970 or equivalent. A dedicated card rather than integrated.</dd>
            </dl>

            <p class="rf-meta">
                These are estimates for about 25 players. More players than that wants more machine.
                Tell us what you are running on
                <a href="https://discord.gg/gtfDts7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord</a> and
                we will sharpen these numbers.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rf-prose">
            <h2 class="rf-panel__title">Worth knowing</h2>
            <ul class="rf-tips">
                <li><b>The client has to be running</b> for characters to earn experience, even when your stream is offline.</li>
                <li><b>It does not have to be on screen.</b> Plenty of streamers run it offscreen and bring it up for raids and dungeons.</li>
                <li><b>Viewers can play while you are offline</b> by typing in <span class="rf-cmd">twitch.tv/popout/[username]/chat</span>.</li>
                <li><b>Free camera is Ctrl and Tab</b>, which is what you need to set town bonuses.</li>
                <li><b>Town plots boost a skill</b> for everyone playing on your stream. Assign them in free camera at the Home village, or from your <a href="/town">town page</a>.</li>
                <li><b>Do not spread your town too thin.</b> On a new server, putting everything into one skill is usually better than a little of each.</li>
                <li><b>The music is public domain</b>, so you are free to use it on stream.</li>
                <li><b>If the player list size resets</b> after a restart, open the UI settings and it comes back.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="rf-prose">
            <h2 class="rf-panel__title">If you get stuck</h2>
            <p>
                The <a href="https://wiki.ravenfall.stream/index.php/Streamer_Setup_Guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">community wiki setup guide</a>
                goes further than this page and is kept up to date by players, and
                <a href="https://discord.gg/gtfDts7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord</a> is
                the fastest way to get an answer from someone who has hit the same thing.
            </p>
            <div class="rf-actions">
                <a class="rf-btn" href="/how-to-play">What your viewers type</a>
                <a class="rf-btn" href="/commands">The full command list</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        </div>
    </div>
</div>
